Last year's &zwnj;iPhone 17 Pro&zwnj; brought one of the biggest design changes to the lineup in years, switching from a titanium frame and glass back to an aluminum unibody with Ceramic Shield 2. With that overhaul out of the way, this year's update is more about what's inside the phone than a new look, focused on the chip, camera, and battery rather than another redesign. The &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max, which shares nearly all of these changes, also gets a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/iphone-18-pro-max-battery-capacities/">battery capacity increase</a> of its own, delivering the largest jump in battery life in the iPhone's history.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/iphone-18-pro-pricing/">starts at &#36;1,199</a> for 256GB, up from the &zwnj;iPhone 17 Pro&zwnj;'s &#36;1,099 starting price. Our guide breaks down every difference between the two generations to help you decide whether the upgrade is worth it. Here's everything that differs between the &zwnj;iPhone 17 Pro&zwnj; and &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;:<br/>
